    <string name="edu_marketCap_title">What is market cap?</string>
    <string name="edu_marketCap_body">Market cap is the total value of all of a company's shares: share price × number of shares. %1$s's market cap is %2$s. It describes size, not whether the shares are cheap or expensive.</string>
    <string name="edu_pe_title">What is P/E?</string>
    <string name="edu_pe_body">P/E compares the share price with a year of earnings per share. A P/E of %2$s means investors pay about $%3$s for every $1 %1$s earned over the last year. A higher or lower P/E alone doesn't mean a stock should be bought or sold.</string>
    <string name="edu_pe_missing">P/E compares the share price with a year of earnings per share. It isn't meaningful when a company has no positive earnings, so StockSteps doesn't show one for %1$s right now.</string>
    <string name="edu_revenueGrowth_title">What is revenue growth?</string>
    <string name="edu_revenueGrowth_body">Revenue is the money a company brings in from sales. %1$s's revenue changed %2$s compared with the year before. Growth shows direction, not profit.</string>
    <string name="edu_dividendYield_title">What is dividend yield?</string>
    <string name="edu_dividendYield_body">Dividend yield is the yearly cash dividend divided by the share price. For %1$s it is %2$s. Some companies pay no dividend and reinvest their earnings instead.</string>
    <string name="edu_unavailable">This value isn't available for %1$s right now.</string>
